\u221a13 /c\u221a13=2. Which satisfies the given ratio. So t= -2 is acceptable.\n\nSimilarly, t=2: a/c=2, so a=2c. Let's check.\n\nPoints A=(2c,0), B=(b,b), C=(c,2c). Check collinearity.\n\nSlope from A to B: (b -0)/(b -2c) =b/(b -2c)\n\nSlope from B to C: (2c -b)/(c -b)\n\nSet equal:\n\nb/(b -2c) = (2c -b)/(c -b)\n\nCross multiply:\n\nb(c -b) = (2c -b)(b -2c)\n\nLeft: bc -b\u00b2\n\nRight: (2c)(b) - (2c)(2c) -b(b) +b(2c)=2bc -4c\u00b2 -b\u00b2 +2bc=4bc -4c\u00b2 -b\u00b2\n\nThus:\n\nbc -b\u00b2 =4bc -4c\u00b2 -b\u00b2\n\nSimplify: bc -b\u00b2 -4bc +4c\u00b2 +b\u00b2= -3bc +4c\u00b2=0 => -3b +4c=0 => b=(4c)/3\n\nThus, if a=2c, then b=4c/3. Then check ratio AB/BC.\n\nCompute AB distance:\n\nsqrt[(4c/3 -2c)^2 + (4c/3 -0)^2] = sqrt[(-2c/3)^2 + (4c/3)^2] = sqrt[(4c\u00b2/9)+(16c\u00b2/9)]=sqrt[20c\u00b2/9]=(2c\u221a5)/3\n\nBC distance:\n\nsqrt[(c -4c/3)^2 + (2c -4c/3)^2] = sqrt[( -c/3)^2 + (2c/3)^2] = sqrt[c\u00b2/9 +4c\u00b2/9]=sqrt[5c\u00b2/9]= (c\u221a5)/3\n\nThus, AB/BC=(2c\u221a5)/3 / (c\u221a5)/3=2. Which satisfies the ratio. So both t=2 and t=-2 are valid.\n\nTherefore, we have two cases: a=2c and a=-2c.\n\nSo, depending on the value of t, we can proceed.\n\nBut the problem doesn't specify any particular positions, so both cases might be possible. However, we need to check if in both cases the rest of the problem setup is valid.\n\nLet me see. If a=2c, then point A=(2c,0), C=(c,2c). Then D=(a,a)=(2c,2c). So D is (2c,2c). Then, the circumcircle of ADC.\n\nWait, points A=(2c,0), D=(2c,2c), and C=(c,2c). These three points.\n\nWait, point A is (2c,0), D is (2c,2c), so they are vertical line x=2c. Then point C is (c,2c). So triangle ADC has vertices at (2c,0), (2c,2c), and (c,2c). So this is a right triangle? Because AD is vertical from (2c,0) to (2c,2c), and DC is horizontal from (2c,2c) to (c,2c). So the right angle is at D. Thus, the circumcircle of a right triangle has the hypotenuse as its diameter. The hypotenuse is AC. So the midpoint of AC is the center.\n\nW