8 = arctan(3/2), same as for f1(\u03b8).\n\nTherefore, both cases have their minimum at \u03b8 = arctan(3/2), with value \u221a39. Therefore, the minimal value of the original expression is \u221a39.\n\nWait, but hold on. Let me verify that. Because when I computed the value at \u03b8 = arctan(3/2), both Case 1 and Case 2 gave the same value \u221a39. So, the entire expression, which is the sum of the two absolute values divided by sqrt(a\u00b2 + b\u00b2), reduces to \u221a39 when a and b are chosen such that \u03b8 = arctan(3/2). Therefore, the minimal value is \u221a39.\n\nBut let me check if this is indeed the minimal value. Since both cases lead to the same value at \u03b8 = arctan(3/2), and in their respective intervals, the function doesn't go lower than that, then yes, the minimal value is \u221a39.\n\nBut to confirm, let me try plugging in specific values of a and b. For example, take \u03b8 = arctan(3/2). Then, a = r cos\u03b8, b = r sin\u03b8, with tan\u03b8 = 3/2, so b/a = 3/2 => b = (3/2)a. Let me set a = 2, b = 3 (so that the ratio is 3/2). Then, compute the expression:\n\nNumerator:\n\n|6*2 - 4*3| + |3(2 + 3\u221a3) + 2(2\u221a3 - 3)|\n\nCompute each term:\n\nFirst term: |12 - 12| = |0| = 0\n\nSecond term: |3*(2 + 3\u221a3) + 2*(2\u221a3 - 3)|\n\nCalculate inside the absolute value:\n\n3*2 + 3*3\u221a3 + 2*2\u221a3 - 2*3\n\n= 6 + 9\u221a3 + 4\u221a3 - 6\n\n= (6 - 6) + (9\u221a3 + 4\u221a3)\n\n= 13\u221a3\n\nSo, numerator is 0 + 13\u221a3 = 13\u221a3\n\nDenominator: sqrt(2\u00b2 + 3\u00b2) = sqrt(4 + 9) = sqrt(13)\n\nTherefore, the expression is 13\u221a3 / sqrt(13) = sqrt(13)*\u221a3 = sqrt(39) \u22486.245, which matches our previous result.\n\nTherefore, this gives the minimal value. But let me check another point to see if we can get a lower value. For example, take \u03b8 = 45 degrees (a = b). Let's compute the expression.\n\nLet a = b = 1.\n\nFirst term: |6*1 - 4*1| = |2| = 2\n\nSecond term: |3(1 + 1\u221a3) + 2(1\u221a3 -1)|\n\nCompute inside:\n\n