h different q's, they are still the same n. Therefore, we need to find all n's in the range 7301 to 16003 such that n/1000 is a rational square, which can be written as (p/q)\u00b2 with q dividing 10 (since q can be 1,2,5,10 as we saw earlier). But perhaps there's a better way to approach this.\n\nAlternatively, since n/1000 must be a perfect square of a rational number, which can be written as (k/10)^2, where k is an integer. Because 1000 = 10^3, so if we write n/1000 = (k/10)^2, then n = (k\u00b2)/100. But n must be of the form m/1000, but wait, n is an integer. Wait, maybe this is another approach. Let's see:\n\nIf sqrt(n/1000) is rational, then n/1000 must be a perfect square. Let me denote sqrt(n/1000) = a/b, where a and b are coprime integers. Then, squaring both sides, we have n/1000 = a\u00b2/b\u00b2 \u21d2 n = (1000a\u00b2)/b\u00b2. Since n must be an integer, b\u00b2 divides 1000a\u00b2. Because a and b are coprime, b\u00b2 divides 1000. Therefore, possible values of b\u00b2 are the square divisors of 1000. As before, 1000 = 2\u00b3 * 5\u00b3. The square divisors must have exponents even numbers less than or equal to the exponents in 1000. Therefore, possible exponents for 2 are 0 or 2 (since 3 is the maximum, and we need even exponents). Similarly, for 5: 0 or 2. Therefore, square divisors are 1, 4, 25, 100. Therefore, b can be 1, 2, 5, 10. Therefore, the possible values of b are 1, 2, 5, 10. Then, n = (1000a\u00b2)/b\u00b2. Therefore, for each possible b, we can find a such that n is in the range [7301, 16003].\n\nSo let's handle each case:\n\n1. b=1: Then n = 1000a\u00b2. As before, n must be between 7301 and 16003. So a\u00b2 must be between 7301/1000 \u22487.301 and 16003/1000\u224816.003. So a can be 3 (a\u00b2=9) and 4 (a\u00b2=16). Thus, n=9000 and 16000.\n\n2. b=2: Then n = (1000a\u00b2)/4 = 250a\u00b2. n must be in [7301,16003]. Then a\u00b2 must be in [7301/250\u224829.204, 16003/250\u224864.012]. So a can be 6 (a\u00b2=36),7 (49),8(64). So n=250*36=9000, 250*49=12250, 250*64=16000.\n\n3. b=5: Then n = (1000a\u00b2)/25 =40a\u00b2. a\u00b2 must be in [7301/40\u2248182.525,16003/40\u2248400.075]. So a can be 14 (196),15(225),16(256),17(289),18(324),19(361),20(400). Thus, n=40*196=7840, 40*225=9000, 40*256=10240, 40*289=11560, 40*324=12960, 40*361=14440,40*400=16000.\n\n4. b=10: Then n=(1000a\u00b2)/100=10a\u00b2. So n=10a\u00b2. Then a\u00b2 must be in [7301/10\u2248730.1,16003/10\u22481600.3]. So a can be from ceiling(sqrt(730.1)) to floor(sqrt(1600.3)). sqrt(730.1)\u224827.03, so a starts at 28. sqrt(1600.3)\u224840.004, so a ends at 40. Therefore, a ranges from 28 to 40 inclusive.\n\nCompute n=10a\u00b2 for a=28 to 40:\n\na=28: 10*784=7840\n\na=29:10*841=8410\n\na=30:10*900=9000\n\na=31:10*961=9610\n\na=32:10*1024=10240\n\na=33:10*1089=10890\n\na=34:10*1156=11560\n\na=35:10*1225=12250\n\na=36:10*1296=12960\n\na=37:10*1369=13690\n\na=38:10*1444=14440\n\na=39:10*1521=15210\n\na=40:10*1600=16000\n\nNow check if these n's are within 7301-16003. Yes, starting from 7840 up to 16000. So these are all valid. Therefore, n=7840,8410,9000,9610,10240,10890,11560,12250,12960,13690,14440,15210,16000.\n\nCalculating sqrt(n/1000) for these:\n\nsqrt(7840/1000)=sqrt(7.84)=2.8\n\nsqrt(8410/1000)=sqrt(8.41)=2.9\n\nsqrt(9000/1000)=3\n\nsqrt(9610/1000)=sqrt(9.61)=3.1\n\nsqrt(10240/1000)=sqrt(10.24)=3.2\n\nsqrt(10890/1000)=sqrt(10.89)=3.3\n\nsqrt(11560/1000)=sqrt(11.56)=3.4\n\nsqrt(12250/1000)=sqrt(12.25)=3.5\n\nsqrt(12960/1000)=sqrt(12.96)=3.6\n\nsqrt(13690/1000)=sqrt(13.69)=3.7\n\nsqrt(14440/1000)=sqrt(14.44)=3.8\n\nsqrt(15210/1000)=sqrt(15.21)=3.9\n\nsqrt(16000/1000)=sqrt(16)=4\n\nAll of these are rational numbers. However, some of these n's have already been found in previous cases. For example, n=7840,9000,10240,11560,12250,12960,14440,16000 were found in previous q cases. The new ones here are 8410,9610,10890,13690,15210. Each of these correspond to new rational numbers: 2.9,3.1,3.3,3.7,3.9.\n\nSo, in total, for b=10, we 