ight angle at \\(H\\), ensuring \\(T\\) lies on circle \\(\\omega\\), the circumcircle of \\(\\triangle PHD\\).\n\n4. **Position and Nature of Quadrilaterals**:\n   - Since \\(T\\) lies on \\(\\omega\\), \\(\\angle PQT = \\angle PHT = 90^\\circ\\), implies \\(PQTD\\) forms a rectangle.\n\n5. **Symmetry and Midpoint Perpendicular Bisector**:\n   - Considering the common perpendicular bisector, \\(\\ell\\), of segments \\(DT\\) and \\(PQ\\) intersecting at \\(O\\), we recognize it as a diameter line which must also pass through the midpoint (orthogonally bisecting line segment \\(AC\\)).\n\n6. **Conclusion on Segment Lengths**:\n   - Since \\(\\ell\\) is the perpendicular bisector of both \\(DT\\) and \\(PQ\\), it is also the perpendicular bisector of \\(AC\\). \n   - Consequently, segments \\(AP\\) and \\(CQ\\) being symmetrically placed relative to \\(\\ell\\) are equal.\n\nThus, we have established that \\(AP = CQ\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{AP = CQ}\n\\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Find all pairs of integers \\((x, y)\\) such that \\(1 + 2^{x} + 2^{2x+1} = y^{2}\\).", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to find all integer pairs (x, y) that satisfy the equation 1 + 2^x + 2^{2x+1} = y\u00b2. Let me start by understanding the equation better. \n\nFirst, let's rewrite the equation for clarity:\n\n1 + 2^x + 2^{2x+1} = y\u00b2\n\nHmm, maybe I can simplify the left side a bit. Let's see, 2^{2x+1} can be written as 2*2^{2x} because 2^{2x+1} = 2^1 * 2^{2x}. So the equation becomes:\n\n1 + 2^x + 2*(2^{2x}) = y\u00b2\n\nThat simplifies to:\n\n1 + 2^x + 2^{2x + 1} = y\u00b2\n\nWait, that's the same as the original. Maybe another approach. Let me denote t = 2^x. Since x is an integer, t will be a power of 2, so t must be a positive integer (since 2^x is always positive). Then, 2^{2x} is t\u00b2, right? So substituting t into the equation:\n\n1 + t + 2*t\u00b2 = y\u00b2\n\nSo the equation becomes 2t\u00b2 + t 