herefore, when we take f(b) modulo b^{n}, we get c_n b^n mod b^{n} = 0, plus the lower terms. Wait, no. Wait, modulo b^{n} would give us the remainder when divided by b^{n}, which is exactly the sum of the lower terms: c_{n-1}b^{n-1} + ... + c_0. But since f(b) = g(b), their remainders modulo b^{n} must be equal. However, that doesn't directly help with the leading coefficient.\n\nAlternatively, consider dividing f(b) by b^{n}. The quotient would be c_n + (c_{n-1}/b + ... + c_0 / b^{n}), but since c_{n-1}, ..., c_0 are integers and b is an integer greater than m \u2265 c_i, the terms (c_{n-1}/b + ... + c_0 / b^{n}) would be less than (m/b + ... + m / b^{n}) < m/(b - 1) < 1, since b > m. Therefore, the integer quotient when dividing f(b) by b^{n} is exactly c_n. Similarly, for g(b), the quotient when dividing by b^{n} is d_n. Since f(b) = g(b), then c_n = d_n. Therefore, the leading coefficients must be equal.\n\nThen, subtract c_n x^n from both f(x) and g(x) to get polynomials f_1(x) = f(x) - c_nx^n and g_1(x) = g(x) - d_nx^n = g(x) - c_nx^n. These are polynomials of degree at most n - 1, with nonnegative integer coefficients. The largest coefficient of f_1 is at most m (since we subtracted a nonnegative term). Now, f_1(a) = f(a) - c_n a^n = g(a) - c_n a^n = g_1(a). Similarly, f_1(b) = g_1(b). Since b > m, and the largest coefficient of f_1 is \u2264 m < b, we can apply the induction hypothesis to f_1 and g_1, which are polynomials of degree < n that agree at two points a < b with b > m. Therefore, by induction, f_1 = g_1, so their coefficients are equal. Hence, f = g.\n\nTherefore, this induction approach works, using both evaluations at a and b to allow the induction step. Wait, but in the induction step, after establishing that c_n = d_n, we only needed that f_1 and g_1 agree at a and b. So even if we started with only f(b) = g(b), we could still get c_n = d_n, but then to apply induction, we need that f_1 and g_1 agree at some point. But in the problem, since f 