le ABD around OX.\n\nLet me start with question 1. To find BD, I need to find the coordinates of points B and D on OY, then compute the distance between them. Maybe coordinate geometry would help here. Let's set up a coordinate system. Let me place point O at the origin. Let\u2019s let OX be along the positive x-axis, and OY forming a 60-degree angle with OX. So coordinates can be assigned accordingly.\n\nLet\u2019s define coordinate system: O is (0,0). OX is along the x-axis. Then, OY is at 60 degrees from OX. So the direction of OY can be represented in coordinates. Since angle XOY is 60 degrees, the coordinates of any point on OY can be parametrized as (t cos 60\u00b0, t sin 60\u00b0) where t is the distance from O. So, in other words, OY is the line y = tan 60\u00b0 x, which is y = \u221a3 x.\n\nPoint A is on OX, at OA = a. So coordinates of A are (a, 0). From A, we draw a perpendicular to OX. Since OX is the x-axis, the perpendicular would be vertical, going up in the y-direction. So the equation of this perpendicular line is x = a. This line intersects OY at point D. Since OY is y = \u221a3 x, substituting x = a into OY's equation gives y = \u221a3 * a. Therefore, point D is at (a, \u221a3 a). Wait, but x = a intersects OY at (a, \u221a3 a). Wait, but OY is from the origin at 60 degrees. But if the length along OY is t, then coordinates would be (t cos 60\u00b0, t sin 60\u00b0). But if we take x = a, then to find where x = a meets OY (which is y = \u221a3 x), then yes, substituting x = a gives y = \u221a3 a, so point D is (a, \u221a3 a). But let's check if this makes sense. The distance from O to D along OY would be the length of OD. Let's compute that. The coordinates of D are (a, \u221a3 a), so the distance from O is sqrt(a\u00b2 + (\u221a3 a)\u00b2) = sqrt(a\u00b2 + 3a\u00b2) = sqrt(4a\u00b2) = 2a. So OD = 2a. That seems okay.\n\nNow, the semicircle is constructed above AD. Since AD is the segment from A (a,0) to D (a, \u221a3 a). Wait, AD is vertical, beca