ightarrow \\text{Discriminant} = 1 - 4 \\cdot 1 = -3 \\, \\text{(no real roots)}\n     \\]\n     Check \\( x = -1 \\):\n     \\[\n     (-1)^{13} + (-1) + 90 = (1 - 1 + 1)q(-1) \\]\n     \\[\n     -1 - 1 + 90 = (1 - 1 + 1)q(-1) \\] \\]\n     \\]\n     Evaluating yields:\n     \\(-(a+2)\\):\n     \\(\n     88 \\not\\mid -88 \\implies invalid\n     \\]\n     Validates not \\( a = 1 \\).\n\n6. Finally, for \\( a = 2 \\):\n   - For \\( x = -1 \\):\n     \\[\n     (-1)^{13} + (-1) + 90 = ( (-1)^2 + (-1)+ 2 )q(-1)\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     88=(-1)(2)\\implies 88= 4-2)) q(-1) satisfying all conditions \n\nThus, the correct value for \\( a \\) is:\n\\(\n  \\boxed{2}\n\\)", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Randomly select seven numbers from the set $\\{1, 2, \\cdots, 63\\}$. Let the smallest of these numbers be denoted as $x$. What is the expected value $\\mathrm{E}(x)$ of $x$?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Alright, so I need to find the expected value of the smallest number when seven numbers are randomly selected from the set {1, 2, ..., 63}. Hmm, okay. Let me try to break this down step by step.\n\nFirst, let me recall what expected value means. The expected value is like the average outcome we would expect if we did an experiment many times. In this case, the experiment is picking seven numbers from 1 to 63 and then noting the smallest one. So, we need to calculate the average of the smallest numbers over all possible such selections.\n\nSince we're dealing with the smallest number, maybe I should think about probabilities. Specifically, the probability that the smallest number is exactly x. Then, the expected value E(x) would be the sum over all possible x of x multiplied by the probability that x is the smallest number. That makes sense.\n\nSo, the formula for expected value would be:\n\nE(x) = \u03a3 [x * P(X = x)] for x from 1 to 63.\n\nBut wait, if we're choosing seven numbers, the smallest number can't be 