.\n   - Express \\(S\\) in terms of \\(s_i\\): \\(S = \\prod_{i=1}^n (2s_i - 1) + 1\\).\n\n4. **Optimization Under Constraint**:\n   - Use AM-GM and convexity arguments to bound the product \\(\\prod_{i=1}^n (2s_i - 1)\\).\n   - Maximum and minimum values of the product are analyzed under \\(\\sum s_i = 1\\).\n\n5. **Critical Cases and Symmetry**:\n   - For \\(n=2\\), direct calculation shows \\(S \\leq 1\\), matching the bound.\n   - For even \\(n \\geq 4\\), symmetry and extremal configurations (e.g., roots approaching specific values) lead to the derived bound.\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe inequality \\(0 < 2^{2}a_{2} + \\cdots + 2^{n}a_{n} \\leq \\left( \\frac{n-2}{n} \\right)^{2} + 1\\) holds by leveraging Vieta's formulas, optimization under constraints, and symmetry considerations. The upper bound is achieved through balanced configurations of roots and coefficients, ensuring the sum \\(S\\) respects the given inequality.\n\n**Final Answer**:\n\\[\n\\boxed{0 < 2^{2}a_{2} + 2^{3}a_{3} + \\cdots + 2^{n}a_{n} \\leq \\left( \\frac{n-2}{n} \\right)^{2} + 1}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Initial setup and introduction of roots:**\n   Let \\( x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\) be the \\( n \\) positive real roots of the polynomial equation \n   \\[\n   a_n x^n + a_{n-1} x^{n-1} + \\cdots + a_2 x^2 - x + 1 = 0.\n   \\]\n   By Vieta's formulas, we have:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^n x_i = -\\frac{a_{n-1}}{a_n}, \\quad \\sum_{1 \\leq i < j \\leq n} x_i x_j = \\frac{a_{n-2}}{a_n}, \\quad \\cdots, \\quad \\prod_{i=1}^n x_i = \\frac{(-1)^n}{a_n}.\n   \\]\n\n2. **Product of all terms:**\n   From the relationships derived through Vieta\u2019s formulas, specifically the last one, we modify and use:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^n \\frac{1}{x_i} = 1.\n   \\]\n   This follows from substituting \\( x \\) by \\( \\frac{1}{x} \\) in the polynomial and considering all roots are positive real numbers.\n\n3. **Analyzing the product \\( \\prod_{i=1}^n \\left( 1 - \\frac{2}{x_i} \\right) \\):**\n   Given that each \\( x_i > 0 \\) for \\( i = 1, 2, \\ldots, n \\), we have:\n   \\[\n   -1 < 1 - \\frac{2}{x_i} < 1.\n   \\]\n   Consequently:\n   \\[\n   -1 < \\prod_{i=1}^n \\left( 1 - \\frac{2}{x_i} \\right) < 1.\n   \\]\n   Using the AM-GM inequality, we obtain:\n   \\[\n   \\prod_{i=1}^n \\left( 1 - \\frac{2}{x_i} \\right) \\leq \\left( \\frac{\\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( 1 - \\frac{2}{x_i} \\right)}{n} \\right)^n.\n   \\]\n   Given \\( \\sum_{i=1}^n \\frac{1}{x_i} = 1 \\), this simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( 1 - \\frac{2}{x_i} \\right) = n - 2 \\sum_{i=1}^n \\frac{1}{x_i} = n - 2.\n   \\]\n   Hence:\n   \\[\n   \\left( \\frac{n - 2}{n} \\right)^n.\n   \\]\n\n4. **Relating the product to polynomial coefficients:**\n   We also have:\n   \\[\n   \\prod_{i=1}^n \\left( 1 - \\frac{2}{x_i} \\right) = \\prod_{i=1}^n \\frac{1}{x_i} \\cdot \\prod_{i=1}^n (x_i - 2).\n   \\]\n   Using Vieta\u2019s relationships:\n   \\[\n   \\prod_{i=1}^n \\frac{1}{x_i} = \\frac{1}{\\prod_{i=1}^n x_i}.\n   \\]\n   We also know \\( \\prod_{i=1}^n (x_i - 2) = a_n \\cdot \\prod_{i=1}^n (2 - x_i) \\). Therefore:\n   \\[\n   \\prod_{i=1}^n (2 - x_i) = 2^n a_n + 2^{n-1} a_{n-1} + \\cdots + 2^2 a_2 + (-2) + 1.\n   \\]\n   Hence:\n   \\[\n   \\prod_{i=1}^n \\left( 1 - \\frac{2}{x_i} \\right) = \\frac{(2^n a_n + 2^{n-1} a_{n-1} + \\cdots + 2^2 a_2 - 2 + 1)}{\\prod_{i=1}^n x_i}.\n   \\]\n   Simplifying:\n   \\[\n   2^n a_n + 2^{n-1} a_{n-1} + \\cdots + 2^2 a_2 - 2 + 1 \\leq \\left( \\frac{n - 2}{n} \\right)^n \\cdot a_n.\n   \\]\n\n5. **Bounding coefficients confirmation:**\n   Hence:\n   \\[\n   2^n a_n + 2^{n-1} a_{n-1} + \\cdots + 2^2 a_2 \\leq \\left( \\frac{n-2}{n} \\right)^n + 2 - 1 = \\left( \\frac{n-2}{n} \\right)^n + 1.\n   \\]\n   Since \\( \\prod_{i=1}^n \\left( 1 - \\frac{2}{x_i} \\right) > -1 \\), it follows that:\n   \\[\n   0 < 2^2 a_2 + \\cdots + 2^n a_n \\leq \\left( \\frac{n-2}{n} \\right)^n + 1.\n   \\]\n\nBy confirming the boundaries and combining the steps, we conc