graph must form a single directed cycle. Each player beats exactly one other player and is beaten by one other, creating a cycle with 12 directed edges.\n2. **Total Games:** In a round-robin tournament with 12 players, there are \\( \\binom{12}{2} = 66 \\) games.\n3. **Draws Calculation:** The cycle structure uses 12 victories (directed edges). The remaining games are draws: \\( 66 - 12 = 54 \\).\n\n**Conclusion:** The number of draws in the tournament is \\( \\boxed{54} \\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\nWe are given that 12 chess players participated in a round-robin tournament, playing one game against each other. This means each player plays against each of the other 11 players.\n\nTo solve the problem, we can use graph theory. Let's consider the directed graph \\(G\\) where:\n- each vertex represents a chess player,\n- each directed edge (an arrow) from player \\(A\\) to player \\(B\\) denotes that \\(A\\) won against \\(B\\).\n\nGiven that each player generated 12 lists, where:\n- the first list includes only themselves,\n- each subsequent list \\(k+1\\) includes those from list \\(k\\) and those whom players in list \\(k\\) won against.\n\nThe crucial observation is that for each player, their 12th list is not the same as the 11th list. This indicates that every player is able to reach at least one additional player by adding one more game, suggesting that the directed graph must be strongly connected. In other words, from each player, it's possible to reach every other player through a path of directed edges in no more than 11 steps, as required.\n\nNotice a specific path:\n\\[ A_1 \\rightarrow A_2 \\rightarrow A_3 \\rightarrow \\cdots \\rightarrow A_{11} \\rightarrow A_{12} \\]\n\nHere, player \\(A_1\\) wins against \\(A_2\\), \\(A_2\\) wins against \\(A_3\\), and so forth, concluding with \\(A_{11}\\) winning against \\(A_{12}\\). Ensuring reachability to every other player implicates that:\n- If a player in this series was able to win against some other player before \\(A_{11}\\), it could reduce the series of connections needed, invalidating the strongly connected property within the stipulated limit.\n\nThus, no player \\(A_i\\) (where \\(1 < i < 12\\)) wins against \\(A_1\\), or it would imply reaching \\(A_1\\) would take less than 11 steps.\n\nHowever, at least one player must have won against \\(A_1\\); otherwise, \\(A_1\\) wouldn\u2019t be reachable by others:\n- By construction and cyclical nature, apparent closer \\(A_{12}\\) must have defeated \\(A_1\\).\n\nIt can be shown iteratively that in the designated constructed solitary cycle, each \\(A_i\\) (except first initiator \\(A_1\\)) wins against the next in order. \n\nThis construction leads to the conclusion:\n\n\\[ \\text{Total individual victories} = 12 \\]  \nRemaining to consider:\n\\[ \\text{Total games} = \\binom{12}{2} = 66 ( \\text{total combination of collected games played in pairs} ) \\]\n\nThe total draws:\n\\[ \\text{Number of Draws} = \\binom{12}{2} - \\text{Total individual victories} = 66 - 12 = 54 \\]\n\nThus, there were \\( \\boxed{54} \\) draws in the tournament.", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "There are 90 children in a chess club. During a session, they were divided into 30 groups of 3 people each, and in each group, everyone played one game with everyone else. No other games were played. A total of 30 \"boy vs. boy\" games and 14 \"girl vs. girl\" games were played. How many \"mixed\" groups were there, i.e., groups that included both boys and girls?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, let me try to figure out this chess club problem. So, there are 90 children divided into 30 groups of 3 each. In each group, everyone plays a game with everyone else. So, in each group of 3, there are 3 games played: each person plays against the two others. That makes sense because in a group of 3, each person has two opponents, but since each game is between two people, the total number of games per group is C(3,2) = 3. So, with 30 groups, the total number of games played is 30 groups * 3 games/group = 90 games in total. \n\nNow, the problem states that there were 30 \"boy vs. boy\" games and 14 \"girl vs. girl\" games. So, the rest of the games must be \"mixed\" games, right? Let me check that. If total games are 90, then the mixed games would be 90 - 30 (boy vs. boy) -14 (girl vs. girl) = 90 -44 = 46 mixed games. So, 46 mixed games. But the question is asking for the number of \"mixed\" groups, which are groups that include both boys and girls. Hmm. So, each group can be all-boys, all-girls, or mixed. The problem wants to know how many mixed groups there were.\n\nFirst, maybe we can figure out how many all-boy groups and all-girl groups there were, and then subtract from 30 to get the mixed ones. Let's think.\n\nIn each all-boy group, how many boy vs. boy games are there? Well, in a group of 3 boys, each game is boy vs. boy. Since there are 3 games per group, an all-boy group contributes 3 boy vs. boy games. Similarly, an all-girl group would contribute 3 girl vs. girl games. A mixed group would have some combination. Let's think about a mixed group. Suppose a mixed group has, say, 2 boys and 1 girl. Then the games would be: between the two boys (which is a boy vs. boy game), and the two games between each boy and the girl (which are mixed games). So, in a 2B,1G group, there is 1 boy vs. boy game and 2 mixed games. Alternatively, a group with 1 boy and 2 girls would have 1 girl vs. girl game and 2 mixed games. \n\nSo, depending on the composition of the mixed group, the number of same-gender games varies. If the group is mixed with 1 boy and 2 girls, there is 1 girl vs. girl game; if it's 2 boys and 1 girl, there's 1 boy vs. boy game. If the group is 1 boy, 1 girl, and 1 other... wait, but the groups are of 3 people. So, a mixed group can either be 2B,1G or 1B,2G. There's no other possibility because 3B would be all-boys, 3G all-girls, and any other combination would have both boys and girls.\n\nSo, for each mixed group:\n\n- If it's 2B,1G: contributes 1 boy vs. boy game and 2 mixed games.\n\n- If it's 1B,2G: contributes 1 girl vs. girl game and 2 mixed games.\n\nSo, the total number of boy vs. boy games is equal to the number of all-boy groups multiplied by 3 plus the number of mixed groups with 2 boys and 1 girl multiplied by 1.\n\nSimilarly, the total number of girl vs. girl games is equal to the number of all-girl groups multiplied by 3 plus the number of mixed groups with 1 boy and 2 girls multiplied by 1.\n\nBut since we don't know how many mixed groups are of each type (2B,1G vs. 1B,2G), this might complicate things. Let me see. Maybe we can denote variables for different types of groups.\n\nLet me try to set up variables:\n\nLet x = number of all-boy groups.\n\nLet y = number of all-girl groups.\n\nLet m = number of mixed groups. So, m = 30 - x - y.\n\nBut within the mixed groups, there are two types: 2B,1G and 1B,2G. Let me denote:\n\nLet m1 = number of mixed groups with 2 boys and 1 girl.\n\nLet m2 = number of mixed groups with 1 boy and 2 girls.\n\nThus, m = m1 + m2.\n\nThen, the total number of boy vs. boy games would be 3x (from all-boy groups) + 1*m1 (from the 2B,1G groups).\n\nSimilarly, the total number of girl vs. girl games would be 3y (from all-girl groups) + 1*m2 (from the 1B,2G groups).\n\nGiven that the total boy vs. boy games are 30 and girl vs. girl games are 14, we can write:\n\n3x + m1 = 30 ...(1)\n\n3y + m2 = 14 ...(2)\n\nAlso, total groups: x + y + m1 + m2 = 30 ...(3)\n\nAdditionally, we can consider the total number of boys and girls. Let B be the total number of boys, G the total number of girls. Then:\n\nB + G = 90 ...(4)\n\nBut we can also express B and G in terms of the groups:\n\nEach all-boy group has 3 boys: so total boys from all-boy groups: 3x.\n\nEach mixed group of 2B,1G contributes 2 boys, so total boys from m1 groups: 2m1.\n\nEach mixed group of 1B,2G contributes 1 boy, so total boys from m2 groups: 1*m2.\n\nTherefore, total boys:\n\nB = 3x + 2m1 + m2 ...(5)\n\nSimilarly, total girls:\n\nG = 3y + m1 + 2m2 ...(6)\n\nBut since B + G = 90, substituting from (5) and (6):\n\n3x + 2m1 + m2 + 3y + m1 + 2m2 = 90\n\nSimplify:\n\n3x + 3y + 3m1 + 3m2 = 90\n\nDivide both sides by 3:\n\nx + y + m1 + m2 = 30\n\nBut from equation (3), this is already true. So that's just confirming the total number of groups, so no new information there.\n\nSo, our main equations are equations (1), (2), and (5) and (6). But since we have variables x, y, m1, m2, and equations (1), (2), (5), (6), and B + G =90.\n\nBut we have 4 variables: x, y, m1, m2.\n\nEquations:\n\n1) 3x + m1 =30\n\n2) 3y + m2 =14\n\n3) B =3x +2m1 +m2\n\n4) G=3y +m1 +2m2\n\n5) B + G =90\n\nBut we need more equations. Wait, perhaps substituting equations (3) and (4) into equation (5):\n\n(3x + 2m1 + m2) + (3y + m1 + 2m2) =90\n\nWhich simplifies to 3x +3y +3m1 +3m2 =90, which is x + y +m1 +m2 =30, which is already known.\n\nSo, maybe we need another approach.\n\nAlternatively, perhaps we can express m1 and m2 in terms of x and y from equations (1) and (2):\n\nFrom equation (1): m1 =30 -3x\n\nFrom equation (2): m2 =14 -3y\n\nThen, total mixed groups m = m1 + m2 =30 -3x +14 -3y=44 -3x -3y\n\nBut total groups are x + y + m =30\n\nSo, substituting m:\n\nx + y +44 -3x -3y =30\n\nSimplify:\n\n-2x -2y +44=30\n\n-2x -2y= -14\n\nDivide both sides by -2:\n\nx + y =7\n\nSo, x + y=7. Therefore, the number of all-boy and all-girl groups combined is 7.\n\nTherefore, the number of mixed groups is 30 -7=23.\n\nWait, so the answer is 23? Wait, but let me check if this is correct.\n\nWait, but the problem is in the translation from the number of games to the number of groups. Let me verify the steps again.\n\nSo, given that m1 =30 -3x, and m2=14 -3y. Then, m1 and m2 must be non-negative integers. So, 30 -3x >=0 => x <=10, and 14 -3y >=0 => y <=4 (since 14/3 is about 4.666). Also, since x and y are non-negative integers, and x + y=7.\n\nSo, possible values of x and y are pairs where x + y=7, and x <=10, y <=4.\n\nThus, possible y can be 0 to4, and x=7 - y. So:\n\nIf y=0, x=7.\n\nBut check if m2=14 -3y=14 -0=14. But m2 is the number of mixed groups with 1B,2G. If y=0 (no all-girl groups), then m2=14, but since total mixed groups m=23, m1 +m2=23, so m1=23 -14=9. Then, check if m1=30 -3x=30 -3*7=30-21=9. Yes, that works.\n\nSimilarly, check if y=1, x=6. Then m2=14 -3*1=11. Then m1=30 -3*6=30 -18=12. Then m1 +m2=12+11=23. Which is correct. But m2=11. But since m2 is the number of groups with 1B,2G, and total girls would be 3y +m1 +2m2=3*1 +12 +2*11=3 +12 +22=37. Similarly, boys would be 3x +2m1 +m2=18 +24 +11=53. 53 +37=90, which checks out.\n\nBut since the problem doesn't ask for the number of boys and girls, just the number of mixed groups, which is 23. So, regardless of the values of x and y (as long as x + y=7 and m1, m2 non-negative), the number of mixed groups is 23. Therefore, is 23 the answer?\n\nWait, but let's think again. The answer seems to be 23, but let me verify with another approach to be safe.\n\nAlternative approach:\n\nEach all-boy group contributes 3 boy vs. boy games. Each all-girl group contributes 3 girl vs. girl games. Each mixed group contributes either 1 boy vs. boy or 1 girl vs. girl game, depending on its composition.\n\nTotal boy vs. boy games: 30 = 3*(number of all-boy groups) +1*(number of mixed groups with 2 boys and 1 girl).\n\nSimilarly, total girl vs. girl games:14 =3*(number of all-girl groups) +1*(number of mixed groups with 1 boy and 2 girls).\n\nLet\u2019s denote:\n\nLet a = number of all-boy groups.\n\nLet b = number of all-girl groups.\n\nLet c = number of mixed groups with 2 boys and 1 girl.\n\nLet d = number of mixed groups with 1 boy and 2 girls.\n\nSo, equations:\n\n3a + c =30 ...(1)\n\n3b + d=14 ...(2)\n\nTotal groups: a + b + c + d=30 ...(3)\n\nTotal boys: 3a +2c +1d= B ...(4)\n\nTotal girls: 3b +1c +2d= G ...(5)\n\nB + G=90 ...(6)\n\nWe need to find the number of mixed groups: c + d.\n\nFrom (1): c=30 -3a\n\nFrom (2): d=14 -3b\n\nSubstituting into equation (3):\n\na + b + (30 -3a) + (14 -3b)=30\n\nSimplify:\n\na + b +30 -3a +14 -3b=30\n\nCombine like terms:\n\n(-2a -2b) +44=30\n\n-2a -2b= -14\n\nDivide by -2:\n\na + b=7\n\nTherefore, total mixed groups= c + d= (30 -3a) + (14 -3b)=44 -3(a + b)=44 -3*7=44 -21=23.\n\nSo, regardless of the values of a and b, as long as a + b=7, the total mixed groups are 23. Therefore, the answer is 23.\n\nBut just to ensure there are no inconsistencies, let's check if possible values of a and b lead to valid c and d (non-negative integers).\n\nSince a + b=7, and a and b are non-negative integers. Let's take a from 0 to7, and b=7 -a.\n\nThen c=30 -3a, d=14 -3b=14 -3*(7 -a)=14 -21 +3a=3a -7.\n\nBut d must be non-negative. So, 3a -7 >=0 => a >= 7/3\u22482.333. Since a must be integer, a>=3.\n\nSimilarly, c=30 -3a >=0 => a<=10, which is already satisfied since a<=7.\n\nSo, possible a values: 3,4,5,6,7.\n\nCheck for each:\n\na=3, b=4:\n\nc=30 -9=21\n\nd=3*3 -7=9 -7=2\n\nCheck if c +d=21 +2=23. Yes. Also, check boys:3a +2c +d=9 +42 +2=53\n\nGirls:3b +c +2d=12 +21 +4=37\n\n53 +37=90. Correct.\n\nSimilarly, a=4, b=3:\n\nc=30 -12=18\n\nd=3*4 -7=12 -7=5\n\nCheck boys:12 +36 +5=53\n\nGirls:9 +18 +10=37\n\nSame totals. Correct.\n\na=5, b=2:\n\nc=30 -15=15\n\nd=15 -7=8\n\nBut wait, d=3*5 -7=15 -7=8\n\nCheck boys:15 +30 +8=53\n\nGirls:6 +15 +16=37\n\nSame.\n\na=6, b=1:\n\nc=30 -18=12\n\nd=18 -7=11\n\nWait, d=3*6 -7=18 -7=11\n\nCheck boys:18 +24 +11=53\n\nGirls:3 +12 +22=37\n\na=7, b=0:\n\nc=30 -21=9\n\nd=21 -7=14\n\nCheck boys:21 +18 +14=53\n\nGirls:0 +9 +28=37\n\nAll these cases lead to B=53 and G=37. So, regardless of how the groups are split, the total boys and girls are 53 and 37. But the problem doesn't ask for that. It asks for the number of mixed groups, which is always 23. Therefore, the answer is 23.\n\nThus, the number of mixed groups is \\boxed{23}.", "deepseek_solution": "To determine the number of mixed groups (those with both boys and girls) in the chess club, we start by analyzing the given data and setting up equations based on the number of games played.\n\n1. **Total Games Analysis**:\n   - Each group of 3 children plays 3 games (each child plays the other two).\n   - With 30 groups, the total number of games is \\(30 \\times 3 = 90\\).\n   - Given 30 \"boy vs. boy\" games and 14 \"girl vs. girl\" games, the remaining games are mixed: \\(90 - 30 - 14 = 46\\).\n\n2. **Group Composition and Games**:\n   - All-boy groups contribute 3 \"boy vs. boy\" games each.\n   - All-girl groups contribute 3 \"girl vs. girl\" games each.\n   - Mixed groups (2 boys and 1 girl) contribute 1 \"boy vs. boy\" game and 2 mixed games.\n   - Mixed groups (1 boy and 2 girls) contribute 1 \"girl vs. girl\" game and 2 mixed games.\n\n3. **Setting Up Equations**:\n   - Let \\(x\\) be the number of all-boy groups and \\(y\\) be the number of all-girl groups.\n   - Let \\(m_1\\) be the number of mixed groups with 2 boys and 1 girl, and \\(m_2\\) be the number of mixed groups with 1 boy and 2 girls.\n   - Total groups: \\(x + y + m_1 + m_2 = 30\\).\n   - \"Boy vs. boy\" games: \\(3x + m_1 = 30\\).\n   - \"Girl vs. girl\" games: \\(3y + m_2 = 14\\).\n\n4. **Solving the Equations**:\n   - From the games equations: \\(m_1 = 30 - 3x\\) and \\(m_2 = 14 - 3y\\).\n   - Substituting \\(m_1\\) and \\(m_2\\) into the total groups equation:\n     \\[\n     x + y + (30 - 3x) + (14 - 3y) = 30\n     \\]\n     Simplifying gives \\(x + y = 7\\).\n\n5. **Calculating Mixed Groups**:\n   - Total mixed groups: \\(m_1 + m_2 = (30 - 3x) + (14 - 3y)\\).\n   - Substituting \\(x + y = 7\\):\n     \\[\n     m_1 + m_2 = 44 - 3(x + y) = 44 - 21 = 23\n     \\].\n\nThus, the number of mixed groups is \\(\\boxed{23}\\).", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. **Identify the total number of games and categorize them:**\n\n   - Total number of games played: \\( 90 \\)\n   - Games between boys: \\( 30 \\)\n   - Games between girls: \\( 14 \\)\n\n   Therefore, the number of mixed games (boy + girl) can be calculated as:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Mixed games} = \\text{Total games} - \\text{Boy games} - \\text{Girl games} = 90 - 30 - 14 = 46\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the structure of games within groups:**\n\n   - Each group consists of 3 children.\n   - Each child plays 2 games within their group because there are only 2 other members to play with.\n   \n   In a group of 3 children, the total number of games played is:\n   \\[\n   \\binom{3}{2} = 3 \\text{ games per group}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Focus on the mixed groups:**\n\n   - In each mixed group (having both boys and girls), there will be exactly 2 mixed games (one between each boy and girl).\n   - Let's denote the number of mixed groups as \\( x \\).\n\n4. **Set up the equation for mixed games:**\n\n   Since each mixed group plays 2 mixed games and we have 46 mixed games in total:\n   \\[\n   2x = 46\n   \\]\n\n5. **Solve for the number of mixed groups:**\n   \\[\n   x = \\frac{46}{2} = 23\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nThere were \\(\\boxed{23}\\) mixed groups.", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "A function \\( f \\) is called periodic if it takes at least two different values and there exists \\( p>0 \\) such that \\( f(x + p) = f(x) \\) for any \\( x \\). Each of the numbers \\( p \\) is called a period of the function \\( f \\).\n\nIs it possible to construct functions \\( g \\) and \\( h \\) with periods 6 and \\( 2 \\pi \\) respectively such that \\( g - h \\) is also a periodic function?", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to figure out if there exist functions g and h with periods 6 and 2\u03c0 respectively, such that their difference g - h is also a periodic function. Hmm, let me start by recalling some basics about periodic functions.\n\nFirst, a periodic function repeats its values at regular intervals called periods. The smallest such interval is called the fundamental period. For example, sin(x) has a period of 2\u03c0, and if you take sin(\u03c0x/3), that would have a period of 6, right? Because the period of sin(kx) is 2\u03c0/k, so if k = \u03c0/3, then the period is 2\u03c0/(\u03c0/3) = 6. So maybe I can use sine functions for both g and h here.\n\nBut wait, the problem states that g has period 6 and h has period 2\u03c0. The question is about whether their difference can also be periodic. The key here might be to consider the periods of the sum or difference of two periodic functions. I remember that if two functions have periods that are commensurate (meaning their ratio is a rational number), then their sum or difference will have a period equal to the least common multiple (LCM) of their periods. If they are incommensurate (ratio is irrational), then the sum or difference might not be periodic at all.\n\nSo, first, let's check if 6 and 2\u03c0 are commensurate. That is, is 6/(2\u03c0) a rational number? Well, 6/(2\u03c0) simplifies to 3/\u03c0. But \u03c0 is an irrational number, so 3/\u03c0 is also irrational. Therefore, 6 and 2\u03c0 are incommensurate periods. That suggests that the difference g - h might not be periodic. But the question is asking whether it's possible to construct such functions. So maybe there's a way even with incommensurate periods?\n\nWait, but I need to think carefully. If g has period 6, then all integer multiples of 6 are also periods. Similarly, h has period 2\u03c0, so all integer multiples of 2\u03c0 are periods for h. For their difference g - h to be periodic, there must exist some common period P such that P is a multiple of both 6 and 2\u03c0. But since 6 and 2\u03c0 are incommensurate, their LCM doesn't exist in the sense that there's no finite P that is a multiple of both. Therefore, the sum or difference would not be periodic.\n\nBut wait, maybe the functions g and h have other periods besides their fundamental ones? For example, if g is pe