sing from 1 to sin(10)\u2248-0.5440.\n\nSo, perhaps we can check each of these intervals where sin(x) is increasing or decreasing and compare it to log x.\n\nFirst interval: x=1 to x=\u03c0/2\u22481.5708. Here, sin(x) increases from sin(1)\u22480.8415 to 1. Meanwhile, log\u2081\u2080x increases from 0 to log\u2081\u2080(\u03c0/2)\u2248log\u2081\u2080(1.5708)\u22480.196. So in this interval, sin(x) is increasing from ~0.84 to 1, and log x is increasing from 0 to ~0.196. Therefore, sin(x) stays above log x in this interval because even the minimum of sin(x) here is ~0.84, which is greater than the maximum of log x here (~0.196). So, no crossing here.\n\nSecond interval: x=\u03c0/2\u22481.5708 to x=3\u03c0/2\u22484.7124. Here, sin(x) decreases from 1 to -1. Log x is increasing from ~0.196 to log\u2081\u2080(3\u03c0/2)\u2248log\u2081\u2080(4.7124)\u22480.673. So, in this interval, sin(x) starts at 1 (which is higher than log x ~0.196) and decreases to -1, while log x increases to ~0.673. Therefore, there must be a point where sin(x) crosses log x from above. So, one root in this interval.\n\nWait, but let's verify. At x=\u03c0\u22483.1416, sin(\u03c0)=0. Log\u2081\u2080(\u03c0)\u22480.497. So sin(\u03c0)=0 < 0.497, so at x=\u03c0, sin(x) is below log x. So, since at x=\u03c0/2\u22481.5708, sin(x)=1 > log\u2081\u2080x\u22480.196, and at x=\u03c0\u22483.1416, sin(x)=0 < log\u2081\u2080x\u22480.497, so by the Intermediate Value Theorem, there is at least one root between \u03c0/2 and \u03c0. Then, as x increases past \u03c0, sin(x) becomes negative, while log x continues to increase. So from x=\u03c0 to x=3\u03c0/2\u22484.7124, sin(x) is decreasing from 0 to -1, and log x is increasing from ~0.497 to ~0.673. So here, sin(x) is negative and log x is positive, so sin(x) - log x is negative - positive, which is negative. Therefore, no crossing here. So in the interval (\u03c0/2, 3\u03c0/2), the crossing happens only once between \u03c0/2 and \u03c0.\n\nThird interval: x=3\u03