00 +0001=1001, which is already larger than 142. So no solution here. \n\nTherefore, there is no a\u2085 such that a\u2085 + reverse(a\u2085)=142. Hence, a\u2086=142 is impossible. Therefore, even though 142 +241=383 is prime, we can't reach a\u2086=142 through the sequence. \n\nHmm. So even if we find a candidate for a\u2086 where a\u2087 is prime, we need to check if such a\u2086 is reachable. This complicates things. \n\nAlternatively, maybe there's a different a\u2086. Let's try a\u2086= 322. reverse(a\u2086)=223. Then a\u2087=322 +223=545, composite. \n\nOr a\u2086= 344, reverse=443. 344+443=787, which is prime. So if a\u2086=344, then a\u2087=787. But can a\u2086=344 exist? \n\nCheck if a\u2085 exists such that a\u2085 + reverse(a\u2085)=344. \n\nLet\u2019s suppose a\u2085 is three-digit: abc. Then 101(a + c) +20b=344. \n\nSo 101S +20b=344, where S=a +c. \n\nPossible S values: 101*3=303, 344-303=41=20b \u2192 b=2.05, not integer. \n\nS=2: 101*2=202. 344-202=142=20b \u2192 b=7.1, no. \n\nS=4: 101*4=404>344. So no solution with three-digit a\u2085. \n\nTwo-digit a\u2085: 11(x+y)=344 \u2192 344/11\u224831.27, not integer. \n\nFour-digit a\u2085: sum would be too large. So no solution. \n\nHence, a\u2086=344 is impossible. \n\nAnother candidate: a\u2086= 76. reverse=67. 76+67=143, which is 11*13, composite. \n\na\u2086= 94, reverse=49. 94+49=143, same. \n\na\u2086= 58, reverse=85. 58+85=143. \n\nNo luck. \n\na\u2086= 34, reverse=43. 34+43=77, composite. \n\na\u2086= 20, reverse=02=2. 20+2=22, composite. \n\na\u2086= 10, reverse=01=1. 10+1=11, prime. But can a\u2086=10? Let's see. \n\nIf a\u2086=10, then a\u2085 + reverse(a\u2085)=10. \n\na\u2085 must be a two-digit number: 10x + y +10y +x=11(x+y)=10. 11(x+y)=10 \u2192 x+y=10/11, impossible. \n\nOr a one-digit a\u2085: x +x=10 \u2192 2x=10 \u2192x=5. So a\u2085=5. Then a\u2086=5 +5=10. \n\nBut then a\u2085=5 implies a\u2084 + reverse(a\u2084)=5. \n\nSimilarly, a\u2084 must be a one-digit number: x +x=5 \u21922x=5\u