divisible by3. Therefore, composite. If a\u2081 is \u22611 or2 mod3, a\u2087 could be prime. But this depends on the actual value. \n\nBut how does this help? Maybe if we can show that for all a\u2081, a\u2087 is divisible by3, but that's not the case. For example, take a\u2081=1. Then a\u2082=2. a\u2083=2+2=4. a\u2084=4+4=8. a\u2085=8+8=16. a\u2086=16+61=77. a\u2087=77+77=154. 154 is 154 mod3: 1+5+4=10\u22611 mod3. So 154\u22611 mod3. 154 is even, composite. \n\nAnother example: a\u2081=2. a\u2082=2+2=4. a\u2083=8. a\u2084=16. a\u2085=77. a\u2086=154. a\u2087=154 +451=605. 605 is divisible by5, composite. \n\nAnother example: a\u2081=4. a\u2082=4+4=8. a\u2083=16. a\u2084=77. a\u2085=154. a\u2086=605. a\u2087=605+506=1111. 1111=101*11, composite. \n\nAnother example: a\u2081=5. a\u2082=5+5=10. a\u2083=10+01=11. a\u2084=11+11=22. a\u2085=44. a\u2086=88. a\u2087=176. 176 is even. \n\nAnother example: a\u2081=7. a\u2082=7+7=14. a\u2083=14+41=55. a\u2084=55+55=110. a\u2085=110+011=121. a\u2086=242. a\u2087=484. All composite. \n\na\u2081=8. a\u2082=8+8=16. a\u2083=77. a\u2084=154. a\u2085=605. a\u2086=1111. a\u2087=2222. Composite. \n\na\u2081=10. a\u2082=10+01=11. a\u2083=22. a\u2084=44. a\u2085=88. a\u2086=176. a\u2087=847. 847: 8+4+7=19\u22611 mod3. Is 847 prime? 847 divided by 7 is 121. 7*121=847. Composite. \n\nAnother example: a\u2081=16. a\u2082=16+61=77. a\u2083=154. a\u2084=605. a\u2085=1111. a\u2086=2222. a\u2087=4444. Composite. \n\na\u2081=17. a\u2082=17+71=88. a\u2083=176. a\u2084=847. a\u2085=1595. a\u2086=7546. a\u2087=7546 +6457=14003. Check if 14003 is prime. Let's test divisibility. 14003: sum of digits 1+4+0+0+3=8\u22612 mod3, not divisible by3. Check divisibility by7: 14003/7\u22482000.428... 7*2000=14000, 14003-14000=3. Not divisible by7. 11: 1 -4 +0 -0 +3=0, which is divisible by11. So 14003=11*1273. Check 1273: 1273/11=115.727..., not. 1273/13=97.923..., 13*97=1261, 1273-1261=12. Not divisible by13. 1273/17\u224874.88. 17*74=1258, 1273-1258=15. Not divisible by17. 1273/7=181.857... 7*181=1267, 1273-1267=6. Not divisible by7. 1273/19=67. 19*67=1273. Yes, so 14003=11*19*67. Composite. \n\nSo even when a\u2087 is not divisible by3, it's still composite. \n\nIs there any way to get a\u2087 as a prime? Let's try smaller starting numbers. \n\na\u2081=1: a\u2082=2, a\u2083=4, a\u2084=8, a\u2085=16, a\u2086=77, a\u2087=154 (composite)\n\na\u2081=2: a\u2082=4, a\u2083=8, a\u2084=16, a\u2085=77, a\u2086=154, a\u2087=605 (composite)\n\na\u2081=3: a\u2082=3+3=6, a\u2083=12, a\u2084=33, a\u2085=66, a\u2086=132, a\u2087=363 (composite)\n\na\u2081=4: as before, composite\n\na\u2081=5: composite\n\na\u2081=6: a\u2082=6+6=12, a\u2083=33, a\u2084=66, a\u2085=132, a\u2086=363, a\u2087=726 (composite)\n\na\u2081=7: as above\n\na\u2081=8: as above\n\na\u2081=9: a\u2082=9+9=18, a\u2083=9+9=18? Wait, no. a\u2081=9, a\u2082=9+9=18. a\u2083=18+81=99. a\u2084=99+99=198. a\u2085=198+891=1089. a\u2086=1089+9801=10890. a\u2087=10890+09801=10890+9801=20691. Check if 20691 is prime. Sum digits:2+0+6+9+1=18\u22610 mod3. Therefore, divisible by3. Composite. \n\na\u2081=10: as before\n\na\u2081=101: Let's try a starting number with three digits. a\u2081=101. a\u2082=101+101=202. a\u2083=404. a\u2084=808. a\u2085=1616. a\u2086=1616+6161=7777. a\u2087=7777+7777=15554. Composite. \n\na\u2081=100: a\u2082=100+001=101. a\u2083=101+101=202. a\u2084=404. a\u2085=808. a\u2086=1616. a\u2087=7777. Composite. \n\na\u2081=121: palindrome. a\u2082=242, a\u2083=484, a\u2084=968, a\u2085=1837, a\u2086=9218, a\u2087=17347. Check if 17347 is prime. Let's see. Sum digits:1+7+3+4+7=22\u22611 mod3. Not divisible by3. Check divisibility by7: 17347/7=2478.14..., 7*2478=17346, 17347-17346=1. Not. 11: 1 -7 +3 -4 +7=0. So 17347 divisible by11. 17347/11=1577. 1577/11=143.363... Not. Check 1577: 1577/13=121.307... 13*121=1573, 1577-1573=4. Not. 1577/17=92.764... Not. Maybe 1577=19*83. 19*83=1577. Yes. Therefore, 17347=11*19*83. Composite. \n\nThis is frustrating. Every example I try leads to a composite a\u2087. Maybe there's a mathematical reason why a\u2087 can't be prime. \n\nLet\u2019s consider the properties of the sequence. Each term is a\u2099 + reverse(a\u2099). Let's consider the number and its reverse. If the number is not a palindrome, then the sum a\u2099 + reverse(a\u2099) will have certain properties. \n\nFor example, when you add a number and its reverse, the resulting number tends to have even digits if there are no carries. But carries complicate things. However, there's a property that the sum a\u2099 + reverse(a\u2099) is always divisible by 11 if the number has an even number of digits. Because for even-digit numbers, the digits can be paired symmetrically, and each pair contributes to a multiple of 11. But this is only for even-digit numbers. For example, a two-digit number: ab + ba = 11(a + b). A four-digit number: abcd + dcba = 1001a + 110b + 110c + 1001d = 11(91a + 10b + 10c +91d). \n\nBut if the number has an odd number of digits, this symmetry doesn't hold. For example, a three-digit number: abc + cba = 101a + 20b +101c =101(a + c) +20b. This isn't necessarily divisible by11. \n\nTherefore, if a\u2087 has an even number of digits, it's divisible by11, hence composite (unless it's 11). But a\u2087 is the seventh term in the sequence. Let's see: starting from a\u2081, each term increases. Unless a\u2081 is very small, a\u2087 is likely to have multiple digits. \n\nIf a\u2087 has two digits, it's possible to be prime. For example, 11 is prime. But can a\u2087 be 11? \n\nTo have a\u2087=11, we need a\u2086 + reverse(a\u2086)=11. \n\nPossible a\u2086: two-digit number. Let a\u2086=10x + y. Then reverse=10y +x. Sum=11x +11y=11(x + y)=11. So x + y=1. Possible digits: x=1, y=0. So a\u2086=10. Then reverse=01=1. 10 +1=11. But a\u2086=10 implies a\u2085 + reverse(a\u2085)=10. As before, this requires a\u2085=5, which is impossible. So no. \n\nAlternatively, a\u2086=1-digit: 1. Then reverse=1. 1+1=2\u226011. So no