red salad is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\dfrac{\\dbinom{4030}{2016}}{2^{4030}}}\n\\]", "ground_truth_solution": "\n1. Define the random variables \\( S \\) and \\( L \\) where \\( S \\) represents the number of people who ordered soup and \\( L \\) represents the number of people who ordered salad. According to the problem, each of the 2015 people independently orders a soup with probability \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\) and independently orders a salad with probability \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\).\n\n2. We seek the probability that \\( S = L + 1 \\).\n\n3. Note that since each person orders soup and salad independently, we consider the binomial distribution for each. Thus, \\( S \\) and \\( L \\) both follow a binomial \\( \\text{Binomial}(2015, \\frac{1}{2}) \\) distribution.\n\n4. Recognize the combinatorial constraint implied by the condition \\( S = L + 1 \\). This means we need to count the number of configurations where exactly one more person orders soup than salad. Given the independence, this combinatorial problem reduces to:\n\n\\[ S + (2015 - L) = 2016 \\]\n\nwhere \\( 2015 - L \\) represents the number of people who did not order salad. Similarly, the total \u201cactions\u201d (either ordering soup or not ordering salad) should account for each of the 2015 individuals plus one additional count because of the condition \\( S = L + 1 \\).\n\n5. We can solve the problem using binomial coefficients and Vandermonde's identity. Specifically, evaluate the sum:\n\n\\[ \\sum_{i=0}^{2014} \\binom{2015}{i} \\binom{2015}{i+1} \\]\n\nThis sum is equivalent to finding the required configurations where soup orders exceed salad orders by exactly 1.\n\n6. We can use Vandermonde's identity, which states:\n\n\\[ \\sum_{i=0}^{n} \\binom{r}{i} \\binom{s}{n-i} = \\binom{r+s}{n} \\]\n\nIn our case, let \\( r \\) and \\( s \\) both be 2015 and \\( n \\) be 2015, leading to:\n\n\\[ \\sum_{i=0}^{2014} \\binom{2015}{i} \\binom{2015}{2015-1-i} = \\binom{4030}{2014} = \\binom{4030}{2016} \\]\n\ngiven by noting that \\( \\binom{2015}{i+1} \\).\n\n7. Each combination occurs with a probability factor of \\( ( \\frac{1}{2} )^{2015+2015} = ( \\frac{1}{2} )^{4030} \\). \n\n8. Hence the probability solution becomes:\n\n\\[ P(S = L+1) = \\frac{\\binom{4030}{2016}}{2^{4030}} \\]\n\nThus, simplifying and incorporating everything above, gives us our desired probability.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\\[ \\boxed{\\frac{\\binom{4030}{2016}}{2^{4030}}} \\]", "domain": "math", "source": "numina_math", "test_cases": null, "starter_code": null}, {"problem": "Given a function \\( f \\) defined on the set of real numbers and taking real values. It is known that for any \\( x \\) and \\( y \\) such that \\( x > y \\), the inequality \\((f(x))^2 \\leqslant f(y)\\) holds. Prove that the range of the function is contained in the interval \\([0,1]\\).\n\n(A. Khrabrov)", "deepseek_reasoning": "Okay, so I need to prove that the range of the function f is contained within [0,1], given that for any real numbers x and y where x > y, the square of f(x) is less than or equal to f(y). Hmm, let me start by unpacking the problem.\n\nFirst, the function f is defined from the real numbers to the real numbers. So its domain is all real numbers, and its codomain is also real numbers. The condition given is that whenever x is greater than y, (f(x))\u00b2 \u2264 f(y). The goal is to show that all the output values of f lie between 0 and 1, inclusive. That means for any real number z, f(z) must satisfy 0 \u2264 f(z) \u2264 1.\n\nLet me think about how to approach this. Since the inequality involves f(x) squared, which is always non-negative, maybe that relates to the lower bound of 0. But why the upper bound of 1? There must be some relationship between consecutive terms or sequences that enforces this upper limit.\n\nFirst, let's note that (f(x))\u00b2 \u2264 f(y) whenever x > y. So if I pick two points where x is to the right of y on the real line, the square of the function's value at x is at most the function's val